### 2. Using Tailwind CSS

This approach is great if your project is already set up with Tailwind CSS. It allows you to define all styles directly in the component file using utility classes.

#### `PrimaryButton.jsx` (Tailwind Version)

This is a single-file component.

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import PropTypes from 'prop-types';

/**
 * A primary action button styled with Tailwind CSS, including hover and disabled states.
 */
const PrimaryButton = ({ children, onClick, disabled = false, type = 'button', ...rest }) => {
  return (
    <button
      type={type}
      onClick={onClick}
      disabled={disabled}
      className={`
        px-6 py-3
        font-semibold text-white
        bg-blue-600 rounded-lg
        shadow-md
        transition-colors duration-200 ease-in-out
        hover:bg-blue-700
        focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500 focus:ring-opacity-75
        disabled:bg-blue-300 disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-70
      `}
      {...rest}
    >
      {children}
    </button>
  );
};

PrimaryButton.propTypes = {
  children: PropTypes.node.isRequired,
  onClick: PropTypes.func,
  disabled: PropTypes.bool,
  type: PropTypes.oneOf(['button', 'submit', 'reset']),
};

export default PrimaryButton;
```

**Explanation of Tailwind Classes:**
*   `px-6 py-3`: Sets horizontal and vertical padding.
*   `font-semibold text-white`: Sets font weight and color.
*   `bg-blue-600 rounded-lg`: Sets the background color and border-radius.
*   `transition-colors...`: Smooths the color change on hover.
*   `hover:bg-blue-700`: Changes the background color on hover.
*   `focus:ring-2...`: Adds a visible outline on focus for accessibility.
*   `disabled:bg-blue-300...`: Applies specific styles when the button has the `disabled` attribute. Tailwind's variant modifiers (`hover:`, `focus:`, `disabled:`) make this incredibly clean.

