This is the core component. It's controlled by `isOpen` and `onClose` props and uses the `children` prop to render any content you pass to it.

```jsx
// src/components/Modal.js

import React from 'react';
import './Modal.css';

const Modal = ({ isOpen, onClose, children }) => {
  // Don't render the modal if it's not open
  if (!isOpen) {
    return null;
  }

  // This function stops the click event from propagating to the backdrop
  const handleContentClick = (e) => {
    e.stopPropagation();
  };

  return (
    // The backdrop div, which covers the whole screen
    <div className="modal-backdrop" onClick={onClose}>
      {/* The content panel, centered on the screen */}
      <div className="modal-content" onClick={handleContentClick}>
        {/* A close button in the top-right corner */}
        <button className="modal-close-button" onClick={onClose}>
          &times; {/* A simple 'X' character */}
        </button>
        {/* The content passed into the modal */}
        {children}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Modal;
```

**Key Features Explained:**

*   **`if (!isOpen) return null;`**: This is the most important line for controlling visibility. If the `isOpen` prop is false, the component renders nothing.
*   **`onClick={onClose}` on the backdrop**: This allows the user to close the modal by clicking anywhere on the darkened background.
*   **`onClick={handleContentClick}` on the content panel**: This uses `e.stopPropagation()` to prevent a click *inside* the modal from bubbling up to the backdrop and closing it unintentionally.
*   **`children` prop**: This is the standard React way to make a component a flexible container. Whatever you put between `<Modal>` and `</Modal>` will be rendered inside the content panel.

