```jsx
// src/components/Modal.js

import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';
import './Modal.css';

const Modal = ({ isOpen, onClose, children }) => {
  // If the modal is not open, don't render anything
  if (!isOpen) return null;

  // Use a portal to render the modal outside of the parent component's DOM tree
  return ReactDOM.createPortal(
    <div className="modal-backdrop" onClick={onClose}>
      <div className="modal-content" onClick={(e) => e.stopPropagation()}>
        <button className="modal-close-button" onClick={onClose}>
          &times;
        </button>
        {children}
      </div>
    </div>,
    document.body // Render the modal directly into the body
  );
};

export default Modal;
```

**Key Concepts in this file:**

*   **`isOpen` Prop:** A boolean that controls the visibility of the modal. The component returns `null` if it's `false`.
*   **`onClose` Prop:** A function that will be called when the user wants to close the modal (by clicking the backdrop or the 'X' button).
*   **`children` Prop:** This allows you to pass any JSX or content from the parent component to be displayed inside the modal.
*   **`ReactDOM.createPortal`:** This renders the modal's JSX as a direct child of `document.body`. This is crucial for stacking context (`z-index`) and preventing CSS issues from parent containers.
*   **`onClick={onClose}` on the backdrop:** Closes the modal when the user clicks the darkened background.
*   **`onClick={(e) => e.stopPropagation()}` on the content:** This is very important! It prevents a click inside the modal content from "bubbling up" to the backdrop and triggering its `onClose` function.

